  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"Zimmermann uses familiar aphorisms as a departure point for this sumptuous meditation on the ideology of day-to-day life in America. The phrases are set in small black boxes against looming, full bleed, extreme close-up images of people in the midst of activities, often only with their hands visible. Highly pixelated and color saturated, you can just barely get a sense of the frantic action: work, leisure, prayer, salutes. Running across the pages, overlapping phrases are revealed, concealed, and combined by the act of turning or unfolding the pages, creating new meanings and adding to the hectic, rythmic, and somewhat ominous overtones of this marvelous work." -- Printed Matter website, accessed 4/26/2012.</subfield>
  </datafield>
